- F-73 **The compiled checker infers a generic's static arguments with coercion: a type parameter that is the whole type of each parameter it occurs in, as `T` in `pick[\T\](x: T, y: T)`, takes the narrowest type that every one of its arguments converts into and its bound permits.** It reads the arguments' static types and the call's expected type (F-87 lists where a call has one). Walk infers with coercion too, from the run-time types (F-137). On both paths, a parameter typed by a type variable that another argument fixes converts a narrower argument: for `scale[\T\](b: Box[\T\], x: T)` and `b: Box[\RR64\]`, `scale(b, 3)` takes `T = RR64` and converts the `3`. So under walk the library's array-scalar `+`, `-`, `MIN` and `MAX`, and the matrix-scalar juxtaposition and `DOT`, take a `ZZ32` scalar with `RR64` elements. A coerced declaration ranks below one that needs no coercion. A tie among coerced declarations is refused, "Ambiguous coercion", outside three narrow cases (the report's section 3.2). A type parameter that numerals alone fix, with no expected type, takes the narrowest default that holds them all (F-342). When the tied declarations differ only at positions where the call passes a numeral, the checker reads the numeral as `ZZ32`, or as `ZZ64` or `ZZ` when its value does not fit. The coercion attempt refuses a call with an untyped function argument (row 507) and a call beyond 64 combinations of candidate types (row 506). Gated by `compiler_tests/InferCoercionShapes`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-inference-checker/REPORT.md` section 3.2; for the converted argument, `reviews/numeric-hierarchy-integration-review/row388-container-probe.md`.
  from: compile-ladder/rung-inference-checker/REPORT.md, "3.2 The attempts, and what each one does"; note: F-51 and F-55 merged in. The types it tries for the parameter to the report's section 3.2; "It builds P-74 and P-49" and "that reading builds P-173's rule" left out (a fact does not point back at its decision); row 506's count of a numeral parameter as one choice to row 506; NatLitArgChecker, CoercionGenericFnCompiledRungC and XXXInferAmbiguousCoercion to the report's section 5. The ranking and the tie stay until the chunk "export" gives them to F-408, the numeral reading until "numerals" gives it to F-342, the array-scalar operators until "arrays" gives them to F-151.

- F-78 **The compiled checker instantiates a type parameter that nothing at a call fixes at the intersection of its upper bounds, never `BottomType`, and tries a call by subtyping without the expected type first.** A parameter with no written bound is bounded by `Any` on both paths (F-296), so one that nothing fixes takes `Any`. A result-only parameter, one that only the result type mentions, takes the intersection of its upper bounds narrowed by the expected type, so where `()` is expected its instance is `()`. Where the call has an expected type, its attempts come in this order: subtyping without the expected type, kept when its result converts to it; subtyping with the expected type; coercion with it; and coercion without it. The second lets a call whose untyped function argument only the expected type fixes type-check, since the coercion attempt cannot check such an argument (row 507). P-49 decides the first and the third; the second and the fourth are a worker's choice, D4 of the instance-bound rung's decision record. An argument of intersection type fixes a parameter at the whole intersection, not at one conjunct. The run time has no class for an intersection type, so such an instance dies as it loads (row 559). Gated by `compiler_tests/InferResultOnly*`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-instance-bound/REPORT.md` sections 1 and 7; the order, by reading, `compile-ladder/rung-instance-bound/decision-record.md` section 2, D4, and the list `attempts` in `scala_src/typechecker/impls/Functionals.scala`.
