- F-242 **`SUM`'s fusion pairs with `MAX` and `MIN` hold no site of the distance, and keep every fused answer under walk.** The fusion pairs are `MaxSumReductionPair`, `MinSumReductionPair` and `SumReduction`'s two `distribute` overloads, through which the team's `Generator2` computes the maximum or the minimum of sums in one pass. A `where` condition on each overload, `T extends StandardMax[\T\]` or `T extends StandardMin[\T\]`, which the checker reads as a bound, meets the bounds that the pairs need. `ActualReduction`'s generic `distribute`, which `SumReduction` inherits, answers a pair over its own `K`, `PossibleReductionPair[\K\]`, so the two overloads, which answer a pair over `Maybe[\T\]`, pass the return-type rule (F-75). The team's commented text, which answers `PossibleReductionPair[\R\]`, moves the two overloads' return-type errors to the generic instead. Under walk, `MAX` and `MIN` over `SUM`, and `SUM` over `PROD`, fuse, and `GeneratorDeclarations` and microGPT's quick pair keep their answers. A `SUM` of `SUM`s over a generator of generators runs through walk's inference of a generic method's static arguments (F-253). The team's explicit call `r.distribute[\L1\](q)` stops every fused pair under walk (row 744). Gated by `tests/GeneratorDeclarations.fss`. Detail: `perf-probes/where-clauses/REPORT.md` A.1 and A.2.1; `perf-probes/generic-distribute/REPORT.md` sections 3, 4 and 5.
