- F-344 **Under walk, a value of the four string objects is read at its trait where walk infers a static argument, and walk's type text prints each of the four by its trait.** `FlatString`, `CatString`, `EmptyString` and `SubString` are read as `String`, also inside tuple types. So `Cell("s")` is a `Cell[\String\]`. A number needs no such reading: `ZZ32`, `ZZ64`, `ZZ`, `NN64` and `RR64` are objects of their own names (F-379), so `Cell(3)` is a `Cell[\ZZ32\]` and `<|1, 2, 3|>` an `ArrayList[\ZZ32\]`. The ilk still names a string object by its own name, `FlatString`. Test: `tests/LanguageTypeInstancesWalk`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-walk-language-types/REPORT.md`, "What changed and why".
