  from: compile-ladder/rung-walk-open-param/REPORT.md, "1. What changed and why"; note: pilot case 2. F-143 is the exception of F-141's rule, an F-bounded parameter left open: its rule, the open type and mk() to F-141; its reduction claims to F-228, which holds them (the declared type or the join first; row 716, an empty SUM at ZZ32's zero); rows 663 and 825, contested, named in F-141 with a few words; the skeptic's probe of dispatch on an open T to its SKEPTIC.md section 3, finding 3; List.fss:179 to row 825; its three tests to F-141's pattern and rows 663 and 825.
- F-141 **Under walk, a type parameter that nothing at a call fixes takes its declared bound, or stays open where its bound mentions the parameter itself; a lone one with no narrowest candidate takes its bound where every argument is below it.** Where some argument is not below its bound, a lone one, as F-137 defines it, takes the arguments' join. A type parameter that the declared parameter types mention only in function domains, an odd number of arrows deep and never inside a static argument, as `T` in `g: T -> ZZ32`, is bounded by the arguments only from above. Where nothing bounds it from below, it takes the meet of its declared bound and the bounds that the arguments place on it. A bound that mentions its own parameter is an F-bound, as in `mk[\T extends Cmp[\T\]\]()`: a call such as `mk()` leaves `T` open. The open type, printed `OPEN`, is below every type, and every value matches it. F-228 gives how a reduction or a comprehension that writes no static argument is instantiated, and where the open type reaches one. Two more rules are defaults of the walk-instance rung's decision record. First, a parameter that only another fixed parameter's bound mentions takes that parameter's instance, not its own bound: `ap[\T, A extends T\](a: A)` gives `T` the type of `a` (D3, row 592). Second, a lone one whose bound mentions a static parameter has the arguments' supertypes among its candidates, and so takes their narrowest common supertype, never its bound (D5). Defects, each with an expected failure: two bounds that walk cannot meet (row 591); a bound only from above that mentions another static parameter, which keeps `Bottom` (row 612); two upper bounds from two function arguments that walk cannot meet, which fail the call (row 616); a parameter that only the result of a function expression with no declared return type fixes, which takes `Bottom` (row 587); a generic passed over at dispatch when its arguments have several minimal common supertypes, whose repair changes which declaration runs (row 589). Defects of the open type, both contested: where overloads are compared, a tuple, arrow or generic type that holds it admits no more than at `Bottom` (row 663); a list comprehension written at it, and `List`'s range subscript at a stride other than 1 on a list of it, end in `CastError` (row 825). Five of the specification's extracted examples, which `ant testSpecData` runs (F-114), write no static argument on a big operator so bounded and pass only because walk leaves the parameter open. Where dispatch reaches a generic declaration that the static call did not select, walk instantiates a parameter that is a parameter's whole type at the argument's run-time type, not at the bound that the specification gives (F-273); with no static types, it always will. Gated by `tests/Infer*Walk.fss`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-walk-instance/REPORT.md` section 2 and its `decision-record.md` section 2; `compile-ladder/rung-walk-meet/REPORT.md` section 1; `compile-ladder/rung-walk-open-param/REPORT.md` sections 1 and 11, and its `SKEPTIC.md` section 3, finding 3, a probe of dispatch on an open `T`; `compile-ladder/rung-body-arguments/SKEPTIC.md` section 3.
  from: compile-ladder/rung-walk-instance/REPORT.md, "2. The rule as built under walk"; note: F-143 merged in (pilot case 2). "It builds P-48" left out; walk's reduction rule and "That rule is the coordinator's call" to F-228; "the curator's to decide" for row 589's repair to row 589, which holds the repair for the curator; "(walk's BottomType)" left out, one word for one thing; rung-walk-elements section 1 to F-228; in from F-114 its five examples (unsettled) and from F-273 walk's run-time instance at dispatch.

- F-144 **Under walk, `BIG MINMAX`, its four tuple forms and `List`'s `BIG CONCAT` run with no static argument written, and walk refuses at load a reduction whose `simpleJoin` is declared at `Any`.** A reduction that extends `AssociativeReduction` and declares `simpleJoin` at `Any` leaves the abstract `simpleJoin`, typed at its element type (F-59), unimplemented, and walk refuses it at load. One with static parameters escapes that check, which skips generic objects (row 665), and stops at its first join. The tuple forms are `BIG MIN_MIN`, `BIG MIN_MAX`, `BIG MAX_MIN` and `BIG MAX_MAX`. A plain-bounded monoid operator with no static argument is instantiated as F-228 gives, so `List`'s `BIG CONCAT` runs, also over a list of list comprehensions. Gated by `tests/Unwritten*Walk`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-reduction-types/REPORT.md` sections 1 and 8; `compile-ladder/rung-walk-elements/REPORT.md` section 7, which lists the operators that ran once and those not measured.
  from: compile-ladder/rung-walk-elements/REPORT.md, "7. Old against new"; note: the typing at the element type and the lifted static argument to F-59; BIG SQCUP, BIG SQCAP, PrefixSet's BIG UNION and PureList's BIG CONCAT to rung-walk-elements section 7; row 665 read as the history's block of 2026-10-13 gives it: the load check that skips generic objects; ReductionSimpleJoinAtAnyWalk named by no cited report (round-2.md).

- F-228 **Under walk, a reduction or a comprehension that writes no static argument instantiates its big operator by a declared type, else by the join of its elements' run-time types, else at its bound, where an F-bounded parameter stays open.** The declared type is a local variable's, or a top-level or local function's declared return type, for either also where the clause form ends a block. A method's return type, a top-level variable, a field, an assignment, a function expression, a branch, a call's argument and another clause form's body do not direct it. Otherwise the clauses run once into the library's collector, `__elemsOf`, which keeps the elements in order, typed at the join of their run-time types, and the operator is instantiated at that join; the reduction then runs over the collected elements, so each body runs once. A declared type or a join that does not fit falls back to the instance that no hint gives: a plain-bounded parameter takes its bound, and an F-bounded one stays open (F-141). So a `SUM` of `ZZ32` and `RR64` values, whose join `Number` is outside the bound, runs with each `+` at its operands' types. The same holds with no element and no declared type. The coordinator chose this rule among the ways that a probe measured (`perf-probes/walk-plain-bound/REPORT.md` section 3). Defects: an empty clause form that no declared type directs takes its bound or stays open, so an empty `SUM` takes `ZZ32`'s zero (row 716); a comprehension that no declared type reaches is built at the join of its elements' run-time types, which a declared `List[\Shape\]` refuses where its elements are all `Circle`s (row 717); a clause form that is a call's argument gets no declared type (rows 510, 705); `PureList`'s range subscript at a stride other than 1, which writes no static argument, answers a list of its elements' run-time types, where `List`'s, which writes `[\E\]`, answers a `List[\E\]` (row 824). Gated by `tests/Unwritten*Walk`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-walk-elements/REPORT.md` sections 1, 5 (5.3 lists the contexts) and 10, decision 6.
  from: compile-ladder/rung-walk-elements/REPORT.md, "1. What changed and why"; commit: 20474c1449; note: "It builds P-48" left out; body(i: I) to F-285; row 718, fixed, to the history, with its test UserBigOperatorResultTraitWalk; in from F-141 its pointer to this rule and "the coordinator's call" (ways 6 and 7 of the probe); in from F-143 the empty SUM at ZZ32's zero (row 716); in from F-285 walk's strided subscripts (row 824); ComprehensionTypedBindingWalk and IntMapComprehensionTypedWalk to the report.

- F-367 **Under walk a generic function is a value, received at the arrow type of its parameter or variable.** A function with static parameters has a type of its own as a value, below `Any` and below each arrow type it can be received at. A parameter, of a function or a constructor, a local variable declared with an arrow type, and a variable of arrow type at an assignment, a mutable top-level variable among them, receive the instance that a direct call with arguments of the arrow's domain types runs. A generic callee's static parameters are fixed by its other arguments first, so the row lift `rows(rmsn, x)` runs with a sized `rmsn`. Where the arrow type leaves a static parameter of the function open, and at `Any`, a type parameter or an untyped place, the value stays generic and each application infers its static arguments, as a direct call does; the compiled checker refuses those forms (row 1021). An overloaded name keeps its whole overload set, and its type as a value holds its generic overloads, so `pick(twice)`, for an overloaded `twice` with a generic declaration, runs the `pick` declared over an arrow type and not `pick(x: Any)`; no test pins this choice. Defects: an immutable top-level variable keeps the generic value (row 1019); a callee's type parameter fixed only by a value's run-time type refuses the function (row 1018); a function declared in a block with static parameters of its own does not run, called or passed (row 1020). Gated by `tests/GenericFunction*Walk.fss`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-generic-function-value/REPORT.md`, "What changed and why", "The rule as built" and "Runs to report, not tests".
  from: compile-ladder/rung-generic-function-value/REPORT.md, "What changed and why"; commit: 567c36dad; note: the source names (FTypeGenericArrow.java, Fcn.received, EvaluatorBase.instanceReceivedAt, OverloadedFunction.java) to the report's "What changed and why"; "Not built:" left out, a status word; the three tests named by one pattern.

- F-173 **The specification's type-inference chapter states the rule that the compiled checker builds: the declaration is chosen first, then its static arguments are inferred.** A type parameter that a parameter's declared type mentions inside another type, as `List[\T\]` mentions `T`, is fixed by the argument at that position. One that is the whole declared type of one or more parameters, as `T` is of `x: T`, and that nothing else fixes, takes the narrowest type, within its bounds, that every one of those arguments has or converts into: `ZZ64` for a `ZZ32` and an `NN32`. Where there is none, and no numeral settles the tie, it takes its bound, narrowed by what the call's expected type requires of the return type. So does a type parameter that neither an argument nor the expected type fixes. Inference never gives a type parameter `BottomType`. The specification asks for inference of a type that a declaration leaves out, and does not describe it (`Specification/appendices/changes.tex`, "The type of a parameter that a declaration leaves out"). The compiled path departs from the chapter in these ways, among others. Its prelude lacks the coercion of `NN32` into `ZZ64`, so a `ZZ32` and an `NN32` instantiate a type parameter at `ZZ`, not `ZZ64`, which no callout names (row 442). The callouts at `Specification/basic/inference.tex:285-347` and `:448-512` give walk's departures, such as the return type `BottomType` that walk gives a function expression that declares none (row 587). Plain tests on both paths gate the bound where no argument type is narrowest: `InferLone*` in `compiler_tests/` and `tests/`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-instance-bound/REPORT.md` (the bound rule); `Specification/basic/inference.tex:45-125` (the chapter's rule).
  from: compile-ladder/rung-instance-bound/REPORT.md, "7. What the specification settles, by row, and each defect's home"; note: "It states P-46, P-48 and P-49" and "(P-48)" left out; XXXInferPromoteNN32 to row 442; in from F-266 (chunk "export", which keeps it until it runs) the specification's word on a type that a declaration leaves out.

- F-273 **The specification gives the run-time instance of a generic declaration that dispatch reaches, and that the static call did not select, by the POPL 2019 paper's rule.** A declaration that the static call selected runs at the instance inferred for the call (F-173). Where an argument's value fixes a type parameter, as a value of `Tag[\X\]` fixes `X`, the rule and both paths agree. Where a type parameter is a parameter's whole type, the argument's run-time type only bounds it from below; the rule then gives it its declared bound, narrowed by what the call's static type requires of the return type. The dispatch paragraph's callout says that both paths take the argument's run-time type there instead: walk because it has no static types (F-141), the compiled path because its dispatcher is not given the call's static type (row 538). The callout also says that the compiled path does not run the paragraph's second example, a generic declaration beside a plain one, reached through an argument of static type `Any` (row 496). Detail: `Specification/basic/overloading.tex:312-349`, in the chapter "Overloading and Multiple Dispatch"; `compile-ladder/rung-spec-overloading/decision-record.md` sections 5 and 6.
  note: "It builds P-48" left out; walk's run-time instance, and that it always will, to F-141; "(P-48)" left out; XXXGenericInstanceUnfixed to row 538; "yet" left out.

- F-300 **The type group's POPL 2019 paper, its technical report and the report's LaTeX sources are in `Papers/Variance/`.** Its README names each file, the paper's licence and the permission under which the report and its sources are included; the working notes on the paper stay in `research/extracts/`. Read in the tree. Detail: `Papers/Variance/README.md`, "Permission and licence".
